\f1\fs36 \cf0 \cb1 This was not the first time I used this information asymmetry. \
\
\
I had been on dates with women in the city and none of them had taken. I described myself in the post and asked if anyone wanted to talk.\
I added her on WeChat the next day. Her last name was Li same as my mother and 100 million other people on the planet. She was twenty-four, a chemistry teacher at a private high school in Shenzhen, the youngest of four daughters in a family her parents had kept extending in the hope of a son. She told me this in the first week. She also told me, gently, that she did not want to send pictures and do not want to see mine either. She said she wanted to be friends.\
We talked every day. She used my time zone for good morning and good night, which meant she was sending the morning message in her late afternoon and the night message before her workday began. After about a month she started asking me what to eat for dinner, and then eating what I said. It started with her showing me photos of the school cafeteria and complaining how all the food are horrible and she had to choose one and maybe I should choose it for her.\
She had things to tell me. She told me about her father, who drove a taxi in their hometown in the north, and her mother, who had come to Shenzhen to help raise her oldest sister's daughter and was working part time as a nanny for other families on the side. She told me about her sister with the PhD, the one with the kid, the one who was an accountant. She told me she went to her sister's apartment on weekends to help out. The commute took most of a Saturday. She made it most weeks.\
I had told her I have driven taxi before, not a taxi exactly, but Uber. I didn't get into it. But I told her Chomsky and Maugham both thought taxi driver was the freest profession in the world.\
She told me about her childhood house. She sent one photo of it, which I have not seen in years and still remember. There was a chicken coop. There were grapevines on a wooden frame in the yard. Her parents had been farmers before they had been other things. Her mother, in the photo, was not in the photo, but her absence was somehow visible in the way the grapevines were tied. Her mother had tied them. Her mother had left for Shenzhen. The vines had kept growing.\
She told me she was depressed and seeing a therapist. She told me she wanted to leave China. Not in any urgent way. She was studying for the English exams, taking them every few months, getting closer to the score she needed. She thought about Canada first. The United States would also be fine. The United Kingdom would be fine. She said this without pressure, the way you mention a long-term plan to someone you have just met. I understood she was telling me where she was going and not asking me to take her there.\
I told her about myself. Some of it was true. I told her I had grown up in a small city in the northeast and come to Canada at twenty-one. I told her I had failed grad school by failing one class and had spent a year selling internet door to door before I taught myself to code. I told her I worked at a data company now and made enough money. I did not tell her how often, in the door-to-door year, I had sat in suburban driveways watching videos on my phone instead of knocking. I did not tell her my parents had paid my rent through that whole period. I did not tell her about the mortgage. The version of me she received was true in its bones and selective in its flesh. I am not sure now whether I knew I was being selective. I think I did and I think I told myself I was being efficient.\
I was also dating other women in real life. She knew. I told her about each one. Where we had gone, how it had gone, whether anything had happened. She listened and sometimes gave me advice. She would tell me what girls liked, what girls didn't like, what I should say next. She did this without irony, the way a colleague might. I thought at the time it meant she did not feel competitive. I think now it meant she did and was choosing not to show it. There is a way of taking care of someone that costs the person doing the taking care. I did not yet know how to see that.\
After two months she sent me photos for the first time. She had a public lecture coming up \'97 her students, the school board, the principal \'97 and she needed help picking an outfit. She sent four photos. In each one she was holding her phone up over her face. I could see the body. The body was tall and well made. I could not see her face. She asked me which one she should wear.\
I picked the one with the black stockings. I told her she would attract attention, wanted or unwanted. She thanked me and said she would wear it. An hour later she sent another picture from the subway, with her phone lifted again, to confirm she was wearing what I had said. 
\f5\i Let's see who is brave and stupid and foolish enough to hit on me
\f1\i0 , she wrote. I read it twice. Then I read it again.\
That night she had a faculty dinner after the lecture. She asked me what to wear. I picked something low-cut. She tried it on, took a photo, sent it. Then she said she could not wear it. There would be male teachers at the dinner. She would change before she left. She lay down on her bed, took a selfie in the low cut, and sent it. Then she stood up and took another selfie in the dress she was actually going to wear, and in this one her face was not hidden.\
She was beautiful. I had assumed she would be. I had not assumed she would be like this. She had a face that looked, I would later learn, vaguely Russian to many of the people who met her. Strangers had asked, when she was a girl, whether she was Uyghur, half-something, half-something else. She was none of those. She was the youngest daughter of four daughters from a small farm with grapevines. The face had simply arrived in her, the way faces arrive.\
I did not gush. I told her I had known she would be pretty and had not known she would be like this. I said if I had known, I would have told her not to wear those photos at all \'97 meaning the body shots from the morning, which had now been retroactively complicated by what I had just seen. I said I had thought her body was the strong card. I knew now her face was another.\
She replied with a voice message. It was the first time I heard her voice. It was higher than I had expected and softer. She said: 
\f5\i Then what should I wear?
\f1\i0  The Mandarin had the slight rising drawn-out tone that Chinese girls use when they want something. I had not heard her use it before. I think she had been holding it back, the way she had been holding back the face.\
I sent a voice message back. I kept my voice level. I told her she could not wear the low cut anymore. She had to cover herself. She put on a long loose t-shirt, full pants, and a hat, and went to the dinner that way. She spent the entire dinner messaging me from under the table. I doubt she said much to her colleagues.\
When she came home she asked, please please, if she could change back into the outfit I had picked in the morning. I said yes. She video-called me. She undressed and put it back on, posed for me, turned around so I could see her from every angle. Then she lay down in her sheets and we talked about nothing in particular for an hour. She said the baggy clothes had been comfortable. She had not realized teachers were allowed to be comfortable.\
It is important to say what I was doing during all of this. I was paying attention. I was also enjoying it. I was the older one, the more experienced one, the one giving instructions. She was clearly happy to receive them. I could tell, even then, that this was a temperament she had come into the conversation already possessing. I did not teach her to want this. I met her wanting it.\
There is a thing that happens in those situations where a man is honest about what he is enjoying and a woman is honest about what she is offering. The thing is not the same as love. It overlaps with love and it is not love. I was not in love with her. I liked her very much. I was about to become someone who would think about her every day for the rest of his life. I did not know that yet either.\
This was the first night I saw her face. There would be many more, and then there would be no more. The relationship lasted about a year. It ended on a date she and I both knew in advance, two months before it happened. Between this night and that date there were hundreds of conversations and one trip and a private joke I did not tell her, encoded into the name I gave her, that she liked but did not fully understand.\
The name was Olivia. Her surname was Li. My surname is Wei.\
She liked the name. She changed her profile name on the question-and-answer site to Olivia within the hour. I told her one of the things the name meant, which was true. I did not tell her the rest. Nobody knew the rest until much later. She does not, as far as I know, know it now.
